# Period pairing as a finite transversal integral in weight at least two

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.periodPairing_eq_transversal_integral_of_weight_ge_two`
(`0536aa57-b006-40e5-839d-6561b4aff387`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-08.

Proof: submission `d8e0fa72-2053-4a92-9034-d9b55e8e7d80`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $k\ge2$, and let $R$ be a finite right transversal for $\Gamma_1(N)$ in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb
Z)$. If an integrable complex density $D$ agrees on the upper half-plane with the period-contraction
density attached to cusp forms $f$ and $q$ of weight $k$, then

$$
\langle f,q\rangle_{\mathrm{per}}=\sum_{\sigma\in R}\int_{\sigma\mathcal D}D(z)\,dz.
$$

This is the finite-index unfolding formula used to pass between the quotient definition of the
period pairing and translated fundamental domains. The lower bound on $k$ ensures that the
symmetric-power degree $k-2$ has the intended weight.

## Explanation of the source proof

# Unfolding the period pairing

The previously proved theorem `MTT.Cohomology.periodPairing_eq_sum_tiles`
applies to the same cusp forms, weight, density, and finite transversal.
It gives exactly

$$
\mathcal B_{k-2}(f,q)=\sum_{\sigma\in R}\int_{\sigma\mathcal D}D(z)\,dx\,dy.
$$

For clarity, that theorem identifies the determinant contraction with
$(2i)^{k-2}\overline{q(z)}f(z)y^{k-2}$. After multiplication by $y^2$,
this is a constant multiple of the invariant Petersson density. It reindexes
the quotient sum along the finite right transversal and uses the invariance
of hyperbolic measure together with $y^2d\mu_{\mathrm{hyp}}=dx\,dy$.
The hypothesis $k\ge2$ guarantees $(k-2)+2=k$ in this computation.

The current statement's integrability assumption is stronger than needed by
the imported comparison theorem, whose change-of-measure identities apply to
the total Bochner integral. All binders of the corrected target are retained.

This connects the established comparison theorem to the corrected frontier
without introducing a new child or mathematical assumption. The sole theorem
import is already Proved: `8684c66d-79fa-4b66-8ec5-580c49b4d30b`, accepted
proof `4792ba68-18c4-4329-a91f-645c4d08767a`. Its original standalone proof
has only the standard axioms, and both it and this exact-target application
compile with default resource limits.

The underlying quotient pairing is the one in the
[Columbia seminar notes, §1.2, Theorem 1, pp.
8–10](https://www.math.columbia.edu/~dmarcil/Seminars/2021_Spring/Notes/Week4-5.pdf).

